In article <40700001@zaphod> doug@zaphod.prime.com writes:
>On the copyleft;  it seems to me that once software is distributed the way
>that Hack has been (essentially public domain) that one cannot impose a 
>more restrictive license (you are always free to make something less 
>restrictive ;-) any (many, millions?) of opinions on this?

If you write a piece of software and distribute it for "noncommercial
use" and I change it and redistribute it as as public domain, I've
violated your copyright and am encouraging others to. I can't grant
users more rights than you did originally; that's up to you. On the
other hand, I have control over my changes and can further restrict
distribution on them as much as I want...
